## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design edge routing with policy-driven gating and cross-DC deployment, plus observability and security concerns in docker-dca.

## Key Concepts

- TLS termination at the edge with Nginx
- Central policy engine (OPA) integration
- Per-tenant feature flag evaluation
- Cross-DC routing semantics
- Observability with Prometheus and OTEL

## Code Example

```rego
package featureflag.allow
default allow = false
allow {
  input.tenant == "tenant-A"
  input.flag == "new_feature"
}
```

```nginx
# Nginx map-based routing example
http {
  map $http_x_feature_flag $upstream {
    default http://api_v1;
    "new_feature" http://api_v2;
  }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    location /api/ { proxy_pass $upstream; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement cache invalidation for policy decisions?
- How would you test the canary routing?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic, per-tenant tracing in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, including config retrieval, TTL-based caching, and graceful degradation when the sidecar cannot fetch config.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry sampling and per-tenant configuration
- Consul KV as a central per-tenant policy store
- TTL caching to limit external calls
- Graceful fallback to sensible default rate
- Cross-DC deployment considerations and observability impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-sampler for per-tenant tracing
class TenantSampler {
  constructor(fetchRate) {
    this.fetchRate = fetchRate; // function(tenant) -> rate
    this.cache = new Map(); // tenant -> {rate, expiry}
  }
  getRate(tenant) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(tenant);
    if (entry && entry.expiry > Date.now()) return entry.rate;
    // fetch from Consul KV here, then cache for 5s
    const rate = this.fetchRate(tenant) ?? 20;
    this.cache.set(tenant, { rate, expiry: Date.now() + 5000 });
    return rate;
  }
  shouldSample(tenant) {
    const rate = this.getRate(tenant);
    return Math.random() * 100 < rate;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness of per-tenant rates during rollout?
- What are failure modes if Consul KV is unavailable, and how to mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to add a real, cross-DC capability using Docker Configs, a TLS edge (Traefik), and a simple stateful component (Redis). It also touches observability (metrics) and inter-DC consistency via pub/sub.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant rate limiting with Redis counters
- Docker Configs for per-tenant quotas
- Cross-DC synchronization via Redis Pub/Sub
- TLS termination with Traefik edge proxy
- Metrics exposure for Prometheus

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-rate-limit middleware sketch
const quota = getQuotaFromConfig(req.headers['X-Tenant']);
const key = `rl:${tenant}:${windowStart}`;
let count = redis.incr(key);
if (count === 1) redis.expire(key, windowSeconds);
if (count > quota) {
  res.status(429).set('Retry-After', String(windowSeconds)).send('Too Many Requests');
} else {
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test burst traffic and quota drift across DCs?
- What strategies ensure accurate time windows across replicas?
- How would you monitor per-tenant usage and alert on anomalies?
